      "title": "Evaluating the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Using a Substitution"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f01405dd-848f-5b49-94e8-8559750cf670",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{- y - z}{x}$ along the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\frac{t^{2}}{2},\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s &=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}$, then along the curve we have $x=t$, $y=\\frac{t^{2}}{2}$, $z=\\frac{t^{2}}{2}$.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(t)\\,\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^2+(t)^2+(t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{- y - z}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{(-1)[\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)]}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)t^2}{(1)t} \\\\  & =- t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf{r}'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}(- t)(\\sqrt{2 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(4 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\int }_{3}^{7}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot - \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{7}u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{3}^{7} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{6}[(7)^{3/2}-(3)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{6}[(7 \\sqrt{7})-(3 \\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{7}}{6}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{7 \\sqrt{7}}{6} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
